# Duetto No. 2 in F major, BWV 803 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: the second of the four Duetti from Clavier-Uebung III
# (1739), strict two-part counterpoint. A bright, rising subject in
# F major in 2/4 frames a long, searching middle section in the minor,
# built on a new chromatic subject that wanders through D minor,
# A minor, F minor and C minor. The opening section then returns whole
# (da capo, written out), now with a new bass under the subject.
#
# Time signature: 2/4, 149 bars, no upbeat, no repeat signs (Mutopia
# duetto.mid / duetto.ly). The MIDI holds 148.5 bars: the last quarter
# of bar 149 is a rest. I checked the da capo against the MIDI: bars
# 117-148 are note for note bars 5-36.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 84, about 3:35. Track times (listing order taken as
# BWV 802-805): Ralph Kirkpatrick (harpsichord, 1952) 3:11 and (Archiv,
# 1959) 3:10, about quarter = 94; Ton Koopman (organ, Teldec, 1996) 3:35,
# about 83; Rosalyn Tureck (piano, 1959) 3:44, about 80; Matteo Messori
# (Brilliant) 3:52, about 77; Helmut Walcha (harpsichord, 1951) 4:20 and
# Bernard Lagace (Analekta) 4:21, both about 69. Sources: the
# bach-cantatas.com discographies and Apple Music/AllMusic listings.
# 84 follows Koopman: brisk enough for the subject's energy, and the
# chromatic middle section still has time to sound.
#
# Structure (my reading of the score and the MIDI):
#   1-4    subject alone in the top, F major.
#   5-16   the bass enters; sequences, falling broken chords (10-16).
#   17-28  to C major and back, the lines in sixteenths.
#   29-37  broken-chord climax high in the top; cadence in F at 37.
#   38-61  middle section: the chromatic subject, D minor, then
#          A minor; cadence in A minor at 61.
#   62-68  A minor, cadence at 68.
#   69-73  a brief F major sequence (the material of bars 29-33).
#   74-90  F minor, the chromatic subject in close imitation; cadence
#          in F minor at 90.
#   91-105 C minor; half close on G at 105.
#   106-112 back toward F, ending on its dominant.
#   113-149 da capo: the subject in the top over a new bass (113-116),
#          then bars 5-37 again; final cadence in F.
#
# Plan: terraced by section, as on two manuals: the outer F major
# sections full, the minor middle a step softer, its darkest part (the
# F minor chromatic passage) softest, rising again through C minor to
# the da capo. Broaden only into the cadences that close sections.
# The bass goes low (down to C2 in bars 28 and 140): 120 notes below B2
# are lifted an octave by the converter, so the bass's broken chords in
# those bars lose their depth and sit closer to the top.

tempo 84

voice mid 0           # two voices: no middle voice to set back

dyn 1 mf              # bars 1-37: the F major opening
broaden 36-37 0.93    # bars 36-37: the cadence that ends the section
dyn 38 mp             # bars 38-73: middle section, D minor and A minor
broaden 60 0.95       # bar 60: into the A minor cadence
dyn 74 p              # bars 74-90: F minor, the chromatic subject
broaden 89 0.95       # bar 89: into the F minor cadence
dyn 91 mp             # bars 91-112: C minor, turning back toward F
broaden 111-112 0.9   # bars 111-112: the dominant before the da capo
dyn 113 mf            # bars 113-149: da capo
lead 113-116 bottom   # the new bass under the returning subject

rit 4 0.75            # bars 146-149: the final cadence
